ollingreview.org/acguidelines"},{"title":"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/"},{"title":"ACL Rolling Review Proposal - Admin Wiki","url":"https://www.aclweb.org/adminwiki/index.php/ACL_Rolling_Review_Proposal"},{"title":"aclrollingreview/reviewerguidelines.md at main · acl-org/aclrollingreview","url":"https://github.com/acl-org/aclrollingreview/blob/main/reviewerguidelines.md"},{"title":"How ARR works – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewing"},{"title":"ACL 2023 Acceptance Recommendations - ACL 2023","url":"https://2023.aclweb.org/blog/overall-recommendation/"},{"title":"Allen Cognitive Level - StatPearls - NCBI Bookshelf","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556125/"}]},"Based on my search results, I found the ACL Rolling Review 2025 overall assessment scale descriptions. Here's the complete breakdown:\n\n## Overall Assessment Scale (5-1 Scale)\n\n**5 = Excellent:** This study is one of the most thorough I have seen, given its type.\n\n**4 = Strong:** This study provides sufficient support for all of its claims. Some extra experiments could be nice, but not essential.\n\n**3 = Acceptable:** This study provides sufficient support for its main claims. Some minor points may need extra support or details.\n\n**2 = Poor:** Some of the main claims are not sufficiently supported. There are major technical/methodological problems.\n\n**1 = Major Issues:** This study is not yet sufficiently thorough to warrant publication or is not relevant to ACL.\n\nAdditionally, the Overall assessment score is an explicit recommendation for the outcome of this paper, if it were committed to an *ACL venue. This is a composite score reflecting your assessment of soundness, excitement, and also other factors like novelty and impact."],"durationSeconds":7.978441685000005}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LE5h36McmvNxhZPCR8LmsF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the ARR review form scales. Let me compile what I know:\n\n**ARR Review Form (used for EMNLP 2026 via ACL Rolling Review):**\n\nThree main scores:\n1. **Soundness** (1-5): How well claims are supported\n   - 5 = Excellent (one of the most thorough studies)\n   - 4 = Strong (sufficient support for all claims)\n   - 3 = Acceptable (sufficient support for main claims)\n   - 2 = Poor (main claims not sufficiently supported)\n   - 1 = Major Issues\n\n2. **Excitement** (1-5): How interesting/impactful, ranging from highly exciting down to not exciting at all\n\n3. **Overall Assessment** (1-5): Composite recommendation spanning from award consideration at the top tier down to resubmit recommendations\n\nNow I'm setting up the review process. The dispatch template requires two reviewers working in parallel—one focused on methods and experiments, the other on novelty and related work—followed by a structured roundtable with independent reviews, cross-sharing, convergence discussion, and a consolidated report.\n\nI have the paper details I need: it's a 10-page submission with 9 references examining why calibration fails in dialect-aware LLM user simulation, proposing three failure mechanisms. Let me get both reviewers started on their independent assessments. I'm setting up the reviewer dispatch process now, preparing to send out the evaluation requests with the experimental findings and acknowledged limitations clearly documented.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1959,"cache_read_input_tokens":62670,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1959},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2e0fab31-3c97-44af-b8df-c308847e2709","uuid":"6d68204c-70fa-4aef-bd68-6d2d3cad4b80"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LE5h36McmvNxhZPCR8LmsF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"EMNLP 2026 uses ACL Rolling Review. I have the scoring scales:\n- **Soundness** (1–5): evidence quality\n- **Excitement** (1–5): impact/novelty\n- **Overall Assessment** (1–5, with half-points): 5=Award, 4=Accept, 3=Borderline, 2=Resubmit, 1=Reject\n\nNow dispatching R1 (methods+experiments) and R2 (novelty+related work) for independent Step 1 reviews."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1959,"cache_read_input_tokens":62670,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1959},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2e0fab31-3c97-44af-b8df-c308847e2709","uuid":"c8e7767b-0c34-4ecc-a665-bf04b0890ee7"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LE5h36McmvNxhZPCR8LmsF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BtFwzKw5zaSQuQYxW6za5c","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 Findings submission. Your focus areas are **methods and experiments**.\n\n## Venue Scoring (ACL Rolling Review)\n- Soundness (1–5): 5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Poor, 1=Major Issues\n- Excitement (1–5): 5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Moderate, 2=Low, 1=None\n- Overall Assessment (1–5 with half-points): 5=Award (top 2.5%), 4=Strong Accept, 3.5=Lean Accept, 3=Borderline, 2.5=Lean Reject, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1.5=Resubmit later, 1=Do not resubmit\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Why Calibration Fails: A Taxonomy of Failure Modes in Dialect-Aware LLM User Simulation\"\n\nThe paper investigates why behavioral calibration of LLM user simulators fails. It studies two strategies—Behavioral Calibration Prompting (BCP) at inference time and DPO at training time—on Qwen2.5-7B/14B in the τ-bench retail domain. It identifies 3 failure mechanisms (FM1: global behavior suppression—BCP collapses completion to 52.7% of baseline; FM2: cross-dimension interference—targeting one dimension shifts others by -23.7%; FM3: overcorrection rebound—extreme targets cause opposite drift) and 1 interaction effect (DPO reverses BCP direction, 10/12 variant×run). Central finding: safety-efficacy gap—scaling 7B→14B eliminates destructive side effects but doesn't improve calibration.\n\n## Key Experimental Details\n- 4 runs × 6 BCP variants × 20 tasks per condition at 7B (480 episodes)\n- 4 runs × 20 tasks at 14B for scale comparison\n- DPO-003 merged model (LoRA r=16, acc 0.75-1.0, loss 0.653)\n- Bootstrap 95% BCa CIs (10,000 resamples)\n- H1 (bias exists), H2 (calibration ≥30% gap reduction), H3 (no harm, completion ≥80% baseline)\n- Llama-3-8B and Mistral-7B attempted but floor-level completion (5%, 0%)\n\n## Key Tables\n- Table 1: 7B BCP aggregate (baseline completion 0.351, AAVE 0.100, Indian 0.150)\n- Table 2: Directional consistency (FM1 4/4, FM2-pol 4/4, FM2-qrate n.s., FM3 4/4)\n- Table 3: DPO-BCP interaction (base BCP AAVE gap -0.091 correct → DPO+BCP +0.064 reversed)\n- Table 4: Scale comparison (7B FM1✓ FM3✓ vs 14B FM1× FM3×, both H2 FAIL)\n- Table 5: Bootstrap CIs (all directional claims exclude zero except FM2 qrate)\n- Table 6: Per-run results (4 runs, 4 variants each)\n\n## Experiment Design Context (from design doc)\nThe paper covers two imagined experiments:\n1. **7B BCP Multi-Run Replication** (plan_005b): 4 runs completed. FM1 CONFIRMED 4/4. FM2-politeness CONFIRMED. FM2-question_rate NOT SIGNIFICANT (p≈0.18). FM3 WEAK POSITIVE 4/4.\n2. **Bootstrap CI** (plan_005): All directional CIs exclude zero except FM2-qrate. H2 upper bound 28.2% < 30%.\n3. **14B DPO+BCP** (plan_006): NOT COMPLETED due to resource constraints. Paper acknowledges this as limitation.\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a thorough review focusing on:\n\n1. **Experimental methodology**: Is the evaluation protocol sound? Are the hypothesis tests (H1/H2/H3) well-designed? Is the 30% threshold for H2 justified? Are 20 tasks × 4 runs sufficient?\n\n2. **Statistical analysis**: Are bootstrap CIs appropriate? Are the wide CIs (e.g., DPO-BCP reversal [+2, +32]pp) problematic? Is the emphasis on \"directional consistency\" over effect sizes a strength or weakness?\n\n3. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to replicate? Are the BCP prompt templates provided? Is the DPO training described sufficiently?\n\n4. **Internal validity**: Could confounds explain the results? Is baseline completion of 0.351 at 7B concerning (model struggling with τ-bench independent of BCP)? Could FM1 simply be that BCP prompts are poorly written rather than a fundamental failure mode?\n\n5. **Missing experiments**: The 14B DPO+BCP experiment is missing. How much does this weaken C2 (DPO-BCP interaction)?\n\n6. **Claims vs evidence alignment**: Are claims appropriately scoped given the evidence?\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n\n### Detailed Assessment\n[2-3 paragraphs on methods/experiments quality]\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1959,"cache_read_input_tokens":62670,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1959},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2e0fab31-3c97-44af-b8df-c308847e2709","uuid":"3a59e14b-3e3f-40ae-9e9e-1d4c2eadb602"}
