{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LE5h36McmvNxhZPCR8LmsF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EMzjXQYSrSW32TNMFn3MVt","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 Findings submission. Your focus areas are **novelty and related work**.\n\n## Venue Scoring (ACL Rolling Review)\n- Soundness (1–5): 5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Poor, 1=Major Issues\n- Excitement (1–5): 5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Moderate, 2=Low, 1=None\n- Overall Assessment (1–5 with half-points): 5=Award (top 2.5%), 4=Strong Accept, 3.5=Lean Accept, 3=Borderline, 2.5=Lean Reject, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1.5=Resubmit later, 1=Do not resubmit\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Why Calibration Fails: A Taxonomy of Failure Modes in Dialect-Aware LLM User Simulation\"\n\nThe paper investigates why behavioral calibration of LLM user simulators fails. Two strategies—BCP (prompt-based calibration) and DPO (training-time)—are evaluated on Qwen2.5-7B/14B in τ-bench retail domain. The paper identifies:\n- FM1: Global behavior suppression (BCP collapses completion to 52.7% of baseline)\n- FM2: Cross-dimension interference (targeting one dimension shifts others by -23.7%)\n- FM3: Overcorrection rebound (extreme targets cause opposite-direction drift, 4/4 runs)\n- FM4/Interaction: DPO reverses BCP calibration direction (10/12 variant×run)\n- Central finding: safety-efficacy gap—7B→14B scaling removes destructive side effects but doesn't improve calibration\n\n## Related Work Section (from paper)\nThe paper covers three areas:\n1. **LLM User Simulation**: Cites Yao et al. 2024 (τ-bench), Sim et al. 2026 (simulator-human gap), Seshadri et al. 2026 (AAVE/Indian English worst calibration)\n2. **Persona Prompting and Accuracy**: Basil et al. 2025 (MMLU drops 71.6%→66.3%), Hu et al. 2026 (up to 30pp degradation)\n3. **Alignment Side Effects**: Gupta et al. 2026 (bias spillover), Lee et al. 2024 (DPO bypasses capabilities)\n\nTotal references: 9 (very sparse for a venue like EMNLP)\n\n## Claimed Contributions\n1. Taxonomy of 3 failure mechanisms (FM1-FM3) validated across 4 runs\n2. DPO-BCP interaction effect at 7B (10/12 variant×run gap expansions)\n3. Scale-dependent failure severity revealing safety-efficacy gap\n4. Actionable constraints for future calibration research\n\n## Key Limitations Acknowledged\n- Single model family (Qwen2.5 only; Llama/Mistral floor-level completion)\n- DPO-BCP interaction only at 7B (14B DPO not feasible)\n- Single task domain (τ-bench retail)\n- Small sample size (n=20 tasks per variant per run)\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a thorough review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**: Is the \"taxonomy of calibration failure modes\" genuinely novel? FM1 is acknowledged as paralleling persona-accuracy tradeoff (Basil et al., Hu et al.). FM2 parallels bias spillover (Gupta et al.). FM3 (overcorrection rebound) is claimed as novel. Is the DPO-BCP interaction genuinely new or predictable from Lee et al. 2024?\n\n2. **Related work completeness**: Only 9 references is very thin. Are there missing lines of work? Consider:\n   - Broader calibration/alignment literature\n   - Dialect/sociolinguistic NLP work\n   - Multi-turn agent evaluation beyond τ-bench\n   - Instruction-following capacity literature\n   - Other DPO/RLHF failure mode work\n   - Constrained generation/decoding literature (only Khan et al. 2026 mentioned in passing)\n\n3. **Contribution framing**: Is \"taxonomy\" an appropriate framing? The paper has 3 failure modes + 1 interaction effect on a single benchmark with a single model family. Is this a taxonomy or a case study?\n\n4. **Safety-efficacy gap**: Is this framing novel and well-supported? Or is it a restatement of known scaling behavior (larger models are harder to steer with prompts)?\n\n5. **Positioning relative to concurrent work**: How well does the paper situate itself vs. Sim et al. 2026, Seshadri et al. 2026, and the persona prompting literature?\n\n6. **Generalizability of claims**: The abstract says \"our taxonomy provides actionable constraints for future calibration research\" but all evidence is from one model family on one benchmark. How generalizable are the findings?\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n\n### Detailed Assessment\n[2-3 paragraphs on novelty/related work quality]\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1959,"cache_read_input_tokens":62670,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1959},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2e0fab31-3c97-44af-b8df-c308847e2709","uuid":"6ffb0cf3-24af-4d19-bcbb-b3b4ee98ec80"}
