{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Neg9q91eXQChuyjbLrJcLe","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_2/projects/demo_calibrated_sim/literature_map.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"papers:\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # DIRECT-COMPETITOR\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # 未找到直接竞品。没有现有工作专门做\\\\\\\"校准 LLM 用户模拟器的方言行为率偏差\\\\\\\"。\\\\n  # 最接近的是 Haq & Saldias 2026（归入 evaluation），但他们只量化偏差，不提出校准方法。\\\\n  # 这验证了 DemoCalib 的新颖性。\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # FOUNDATION\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  - id: seshadri2026lost\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Lost in Simulation: LLM-Simulated Users are Unreliable Proxies for Human Users in Agentic Evaluations\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Seshadri, Cahyawijaya, Odumakinde, Singh, Goldfarb-Tarrant\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2601.17087\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.17087\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: foundation\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"Documents that LLM user simulators are differentially effective proxies across demographic groups; AAVE and Indian English speakers get worst calibration\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: high\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Agent success rates vary up to 9pp across different user simulator LLMs\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"AAVE speakers experience consistently worse success rates and calibration errors than SAE speakers\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Simulated users introduce conversational artifacts absent from real human users\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Disparities compound significantly with age for AAVE speakers\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"They document the problem; we document why both BCP and DPO calibration fail to close the gaps, providing a failure taxonomy\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: yao2024taubench\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"tau-bench: A Benchmark for Tool-Agent-User Interaction in Real-World Domains\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Yao et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2406.12045\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.12045\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: foundation\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"The agent evaluation framework we build on; provides retail customer service domain with user simulator + tool-calling agent + pass^k metric\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: high\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Even gpt-4o succeeds on <50% of tasks; pass^8 <25% in retail\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Proposes pass^k metric measuring reliability over multiple trials\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"User simulator is LLM-based, enabling behavioral modification\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"We extend tau-bench by documenting systematic failure modes when calibrating the user simulator's dialect-specific behavioral rates\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: liu2024agentbench\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"AgentBench: Evaluating LLMs as Agents\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Liu et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.03688\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: foundation\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"Multi-environment agent benchmark; establishes evaluation methodology for LLM agents across 8 environments\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: medium\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Significant performance gap between commercial and open-source LLMs as agents\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Poor long-term reasoning and instruction following are main obstacles\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"AgentBench evaluates agent capability; we focus on user simulator fidelity and fairness\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: wang2024mint\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"MINT: Evaluating LLMs in Multi-turn Interaction with Tools and Language Feedback\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Wang, Wang, Liu, Chen, Yuan, Peng, Ji\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.10691\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: foundation\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"Multi-turn agent evaluation benchmark; shows gap between single-turn and multi-turn performance\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: medium\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"LLMs benefit 1-8% per turn from tools, 2-17% from language feedback\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Better single-turn performance does not guarantee better multi-turn performance\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"SIFT and RLHF generally hurt multi-turn capabilities\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"MINT uses GPT-4 simulated users without demographic variation; we investigate dialect-aware calibration and document its failure modes\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # METHOD-COMPONENT\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  - id: hu2021lora\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Hu, Shen, Wallis, Allen-Zhu, Li, Wang, Wang, Chen\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2022 (arXiv 2106.09685)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2021\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"LoRA adapter method used in our DPO training; enables parameter-efficient fine-tuning of Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: high\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Low-rank decomposition of weight updates reduces trainable parameters by 10,000x\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Matches or exceeds full fine-tuning quality on GPT-3 175B\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"No additional inference latency when weights are merged\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"We use LoRA as a training component for DPO-based behavioral calibration\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: rafailov2023dpo\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Direct Preference Optimization: Your Language Model is Secretly a Reward Model\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Rafailov, Sharma, Mitchell, Manning, Ermon, Finn\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2023\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.18290\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"Core algorithm for our DPO-based behavioral alignment; eliminates need for reward model training\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: high\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"DPO directly optimizes policy from preference pairs without explicit reward model\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Computationally lightweight, stable alternative to PPO-based RLHF\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Exceeds PPO-based RLHF in controlling sentiment of generations\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"We apply DPO to attempt dialect behavioral calibration and document its systematic failure modes including calibration reversal\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: zhao2021calibrate\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Calibrate Before Use: Improving Few-Shot Performance of Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Zhao, Wallace, Feng, Klein, Singh\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2021\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2021\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.09690\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"Foundational calibration method for LLMs; contextual calibration corrects output distribution biases\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: medium\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Few-shot accuracy varies from near chance to near SOTA depending on prompt format\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Identified majority label bias, recency bias, common token bias\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Contextual calibration with content-free inputs improves accuracy up to 30%\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"They calibrate classification outputs; we attempt to calibrate behavioral rates (question rate, politeness) in generation and document why it fails\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: abdulhai2025consistent\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Consistently Simulating Human Personas with Multi-Turn Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Abdulhai, Cheng, Clay, Althoff, Levine, Jaques\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.00222\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"Multi-turn RL for persona consistency; directly relevant to maintaining calibrated behavior across conversation turns\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: high\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Off-the-shelf LLMs drift from assigned personas over multi-turn dialogue\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Three automatic metrics: prompt-to-line, line-to-line, Q&A consistency\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Multi-turn RL reduces inconsistency by >55%\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"They optimize persona consistency in general; we document failure modes when targeting dialect behavioral rate calibration\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: naous2025flipping\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Flipping the Dialogue: Training and Evaluating User Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Naous, Laban, Xu, Neville\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2510.06552\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06552\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"Purpose-built User LMs outperform repurposed assistant LMs as user simulators; validates need for specialized user models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: high\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Better assistant LMs yield worse user simulators\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"UserLM-8b provides more realistic user simulation\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"GPT-4o performance drops from 74.6% to 57.4% under realistic user simulation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"They train general User LMs; we document why calibrating user simulator behavioral rates for specific dialect groups fails\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: li2025persona\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"LLM Generated Persona is a Promise with a Catch\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Li, Chen et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.16527\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"Documents systematic biases in LLM-generated personas; explains why naive persona prompting fails for demographic simulation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: medium\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"LLM-generated personas systematically drift toward socially desirable traits\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"More LLM-generated content produces more left-leaning, less representative personas\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Bias exists in the persona creation step itself, not just model responses\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"We document failure modes when directly injecting behavioral rate targets via BCP, including persona-like completion collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: wang2024alignment\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"A Comprehensive Survey of LLM Alignment Techniques: RLHF, RLAIF, PPO, DPO and More\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Wang, Bi, Pentyala et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2407.16216\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.16216\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"Survey unifying alignment methods under single policy gradient framework; reference for our DPO methodology\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: medium\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Unified policy gradient framework for pretraining, SFT, RLHF, RLVR\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"DPO is stable and lightweight but sensitive to preference data quality\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Detailed comparison of PPO vs GRPO vs DPO implementation details\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"Survey provides methodology reference; we apply DPO to user simulator dialect calibration and document its failure modes\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # EVALUATION\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  - id: lin2025redial\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Assessing Dialect Fairness and Robustness of Large Language Models in Reasoning Tasks\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Lin et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ACL 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.11005\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: evaluation\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"First human-annotated SE-AAVE parallel benchmark (ReDial); quantifies LLM performance gaps on AAVE\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: high\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"1.2K+ parallel SE-AAVE prompt pairs across reasoning tasks\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Almost all models show statistically significant performance drops on AAVE\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"AAVE performance gaps arise from morphosyntactic features and conversational norms\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"LLMs struggle more with AAVE than misspelled Standard English\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"ReDial evaluates reasoning task performance; we evaluate behavioral rates in user simulation and document calibration failure modes\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: faisal2024dialectbench\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"DialectBench: A NLP Benchmark for Dialects, Varieties, and Closely-Related Languages\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Faisal et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ACL 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.11009\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: evaluation\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"Large-scale multi-dialect NLP benchmark; documents performance disparities across 281 language varieties\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: medium\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"10 NLP tasks, 281 varieties, 40 language clusters\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Substantial performance disparities between standard and non-standard varieties\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Performance gap widens with fine-tuning due to uneven data availability\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"DialectBench evaluates NLP task performance; we document failure modes in behavioral calibration of user simulators\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: gallegos2024bias\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Bias and Fairness in Large Language Models: A Survey\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Gallegos, Rossi, Barrow et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"Computational Linguistics, Vol. 50(3)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.00770\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: evaluation\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"Comprehensive survey of LLM bias evaluation and mitigation; provides taxonomy of metrics and datasets\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: medium\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Taxonomy of bias metrics at embedding, probability, and generated text levels\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Mitigation methods categorized by intervention stage: pre/in/intra/post-processing\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Consolidated public datasets for bias evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"Survey covers general LLM bias; we address a specific bias modality (behavioral rates in user simulation)\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: parrish2022bbq\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"BBQ: A Hand-Built Bias Benchmark for Question Answering\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Parrish et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"Findings of ACL 2022\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2022\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://aclanthology.org/2022.findings-acl.165/\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: evaluation\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"Established bias benchmark for QA; evaluates social biases across multiple demographic categories\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: low\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"58,492 unique examples across multiple bias categories\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Evaluates both individual and intersectional biases\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Standard benchmark for LLM bias evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"BBQ measures representational bias in QA; we measure behavioral bias in interactive dialogue simulation\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: haq2026dialect\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Dialect vs Demographics: Quantifying LLM Bias from Implicit Linguistic Signals vs. Explicit User Profiles\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Haq, Saldias\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2604.21152\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.21152\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: evaluation\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"Closest to direct competitor; quantifies how dialect cues vs explicit identity trigger differential LLM behavior\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: high\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Dialect cues trigger 'dialect jailbreak' reducing refusal probability to near zero\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"AAVE/Singlish users receive less sanitized content than SAE users\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Safety alignment is brittle, over-indexed on explicit keywords\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"24,000+ responses analyzed across two LLMs\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"They characterize the bias mechanism (implicit vs explicit); we document why calibration methods fail to correct behavioral rates\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # BACKGROUND\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  - id: joshi2025dialect_survey\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Natural Language Processing for Dialects of a Language: A Survey\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Joshi, Dabre, Kanojia, Li, Zhan, Haffari, Dippold\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ACM Computing Surveys, Vol. 57(6)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.05632\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"Comprehensive survey of NLP for dialects; covers datasets, approaches, and performance gaps across dialect varieties\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: medium\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"NLP models show systematic performance degradation on dialectal data\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Covers NLU and NLG tasks including dialogue systems\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Equity implications of language technology performance gaps\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"Survey provides broad context; we tackle a specific gap in user simulation fairness\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: rickford1999aave\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"African American Vernacular English: Features, Evolution, Educational Implications\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Rickford, John Russell\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"Blackwell Publishers (Language in Society series)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 1999\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://www.amazon.com/African-American-Vernacular-English-Implications/dp/0631212450\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"Authoritative reference on AAVE phonological, grammatical, and pragmatic features; grounds our behavioral rate targets\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: medium\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Systematic documentation of AAVE phonological and grammatical features\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"AAVE has distinct pragmatic conventions including directness patterns\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"AAVE is a fully systematic linguistic variety, not deficient English\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"Provides sociolinguistic grounding for what behavioral patterns we should calibrate toward\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: kachru2008cultures\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Cultures, Contexts, and World Englishes\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Kachru, Yamuna; Smith, Cecil\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"Routledge\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2008\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://www.routledge.com/Cultures-Contexts-and-World-Englishes/Kachru-Smith/p/book/9780805847338\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"Framework for Indian English pragmatics; documents politeness strategies and speech act differences in Outer Circle Englishes\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: medium\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Indian English has distinct politeness strategies rooted in face-saving norms\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Requesting and disagreeing strategies differ from Inner Circle varieties\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Three Circles model (Inner/Outer/Expanding) for understanding English variation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"Provides sociolinguistic grounding for Indian English behavioral patterns we attempt to calibrate\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: sekulic2024daus\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Reliable LLM-based User Simulator for Task-Oriented Dialogue Systems\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Sekulic, Terragni, Guimaraes et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"SCI-CHAT Workshop 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.13374\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"DAUS: domain-aware user simulator fine-tuned on real dialogues; shows fine-tuning improves goal fulfillment and reduces hallucination\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: medium\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Fine-tuning LLMs on real dialogue examples improves user goal fulfillment\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Mitigates hallucinations in simulator responses\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Domain-aware approach outperforms generic prompting\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"DAUS optimizes task completion; we document failure modes when optimizing demographic behavioral fidelity\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: ahmad2025diversity\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Simulating User Diversity in Task-Oriented Dialogue Systems using Large Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Ahmad, Hillmann, Moller\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2502.12813\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.12813\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"Generates diverse user profiles with varied demographics and conversational styles for dialogue evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: medium\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"GPT-o1 generates more heterogeneous user distributions than GPT-4o\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"82.46% task success across 57 dialogue sessions with diverse users\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Captures variation in language styles, knowledge levels, and objectives\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"They generate diverse profiles; we document why calibrating specific behavioral rates to match real dialect group distributions fails\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # NEW ADDITIONS (scan 2026-05-13)\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n  # --- FOUNDATION (new) ---\\\\n  - id: sim2real2026gap\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Mind the Sim2Real Gap in User Simulation for Agentic Tasks\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Zhou, Sun, Xu, Chen, Radev\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2603.11245\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.11245\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: foundation\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"First study running full τ-bench protocol with 451 real humans; introduces USI metric to quantify sim-vs-human behavioral divergence\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: high\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"LLM simulators are excessively cooperative, stylistically uniform, and lack realistic frustration/ambiguity\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Simulated users create 'easy mode' that inflates agent success rates above human baseline\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Simulated feedback is uniformly positive; humans express calibrated dissatisfaction\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Higher model capability does not yield more faithful user simulation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"They quantify the sim2real gap but do not propose calibration methods; we attempt BCP and DPO calibration and document why both fail\\\\\\\"\\\\n    scan_date: \\\\\\\"2026-05-13\\\\\\\"\\\\n    note: \\\\\\\"Near-competitor in problem framing — directly validates our motivation. Not a direct competitor because they diagnose without prescribing a fix.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # --- METHOD-COMPONENT (new) ---\\\\n  - id: khan2026decoding\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Decoding-Time Debiasing via Process Reward Models: From Controlled Fill-in to Open-Ended Generation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Khan, Muneeb Ur Raheem\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2605.02348\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.02348\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"Decoding-time bias mitigation using Process Reward Model as fairness judge; alternative to our training-time DPO approach\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: medium\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Sequential debiasing raises bias scores by up to +0.40 over baseline while preserving fluency\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"No weight changes needed — debiasing happens at inference via PRM scoring\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Gate calibration is model-dependent; no one-size-fits-all prompt works\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"They debias at decoding time for general fairness; we document failure modes of training-time calibration (BCP+DPO), motivating alternative approaches like decoding-time methods\\\\\\\"\\\\n    scan_date: \\\\\\\"2026-05-13\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # --- EVALUATION (new) ---\\\\n  - id: gupta2025endive\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"EnDive: A Cross-Dialect Benchmark for Fairness and Performance in Large Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Gupta, Cheung et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"Findings of EMNLP 2025 (arXiv 2504.07100)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.07100\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: evaluation\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"Cross-dialect benchmark covering 5 dialects including AAVE and Indian English — both our target dialects\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: high\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Covers AAVE (33M speakers), Indian English (250M speakers), Jamaican, Chicano, Singaporean English\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Few-shot LLM translation of SE to dialects, validated by native speakers (avg 6.02/7 faithfulness)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"LLMs show performance drops on non-standard dialects across understanding, reasoning, math, logic\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"EnDive evaluates NLP task performance across dialects; we document calibration failure modes for user simulators targeting those same dialect groups\\\\\\\"\\\\n    scan_date: \\\\\\\"2026-05-13\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # --- METHOD-COMPONENT (new, Plan B relevant) ---\\\\n  - id: lee2024dpo_toxicity\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"A Mechanistic Understanding of Alignment Algorithms: A Case Study on DPO and Toxicity\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Lee, Panda, Yun, Bansal\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.01967\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"Shows DPO bypasses rather than removes pre-training capabilities; explains mechanistically why DPO+BCP can reverse calibration direction\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: high\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"DPO does not remove toxic capabilities, only bypasses them at inference\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Simple un-alignment can revert model to pre-DPO behavior\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Minimal parameter changes allow reversion — alignment is fragile\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"They study DPO toxicity mechanics; we document DPO+BCP calibration reversal as a specific failure mode\\\\\\\"\\\\n    scan_date: \\\\\\\"2026-05-13\\\\\\\"\\\\n    plan_b_relevance: \\\\\\\"FM4 — explains why DPO training can reverse BCP calibration effect\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: gupta2026intra_fairness\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Intra-Fairness Dynamics: The Bias Spillover Effect in Targeted LLM Alignment\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Paraschou, Heiter, Iosifidis\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2602.16438\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.16438\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"Documents bias spillover — targeted debiasing on one dimension causes unintended effects on other dimensions; directly parallels our cross-dimension interference\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: high\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Targeted debiasing can produce unintended negative consequences across other social axes\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Bias spillover effect: altering behavior on one dimension when mitigating another\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Multi-attribute fairness dynamics must be considered in alignment\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"They study bias spillover in general alignment; we document the specific cross-dimension interference pattern in BCP-based behavioral calibration\\\\\\\"\\\\n    scan_date: \\\\\\\"2026-05-13\\\\\\\"\\\\n    plan_b_relevance: \\\\\\\"FM2 + FM4 — directly supports cross-dimension interference and DPO spillover claims\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: basil2025playing_pretend\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Prompting Science Report 4: Playing Pretend: Expert Personas Don't Improve Factual Accuracy\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Basil, Shapiro, Shapiro, Mollick, Mollick, Meincke\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2512.05858 (Wharton)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.05858\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"Shows persona prompting degrades factual accuracy; BCP is a form of persona/role prompting, explaining completion collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: high\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Expert personas do not improve factual accuracy across benchmarks\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Persona prompting activates instruction-following mode at expense of factual recall\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"MMLU accuracy drops from 71.6% baseline to 66.3% with detailed persona\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"They study persona prompting in classification/QA; we document the same mechanism in multi-turn dialogue task completion\\\\\\\"\\\\n    scan_date: \\\\\\\"2026-05-13\\\\\\\"\\\\n    plan_b_relevance: \\\\\\\"FM1 — explains mechanism behind global behavior suppression\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: hu2026expert_persona\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Expert Personas Improve LLM Alignment but Damage Accuracy: Bootstrapping Intent-Based Persona Routing with PRISM\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Hu, Rostami, Thomason\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2603.18507\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.18507\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"Systematically documents persona prompting trade-off: alignment improves but accuracy degrades up to 30pp; directly explains BCP completion collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: high\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Persona prompting improves alignment tasks but degrades knowledge/reasoning tasks\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Performance drops up to 30pp under persona modifications\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"More instruction-tuned models are more susceptible to persona-induced accuracy loss\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"They study general persona-accuracy trade-off; we document the specific completion collapse in agent-user dialogue under behavioral calibration prompts\\\\\\\"\\\\n    scan_date: \\\\\\\"2026-05-13\\\\\\\"\\\\n    plan_b_relevance: \\\\\\\"FM1 — strongest external evidence for global behavior suppression mechanism\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # --- BACKGROUND (new) ---\\\\n  - id: nakash2026divert\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Efficient Agent Evaluation via Diversity-Guided User Simulation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Nakash, Kour, Anaby-Tavor\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2604.21480\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.21480\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"DIVERT framework for efficient agent evaluation via branching trajectories; addresses Monte Carlo inefficiency in agent evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: medium\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Snapshot-based branching avoids redundant regeneration of early conversation prefixes\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Coverage-guided exploration uncovers deep failure modes from rare user behaviors\\\\\\\"\\\\n    how_we_differ: \\\\\\\"They optimize evaluation efficiency; we document failure modes in user simulator fidelity calibration for dialect groups\\\\\\\"\\\\n    scan_date: \\\\\\\"2026-05-13\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# SUMMARY\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# Total: 29 papers (updated 2026-05-13, +9 new, +4 Plan B scan)\\\\n# - direct-competitor: 0 (novelty still confirmed — no one has done systematic BCP failure analysis)\\\\n# - foundation: 5 (+sim2real2026gap)\\\\n# - method-component: 13 (+hu2021lora, +khan2026decoding, +lee2024dpo_toxicity, +gupta2026intra_fairness, +basil2025playing_pretend, +hu2026expert_persona)\\\\n# - evaluation: 6 (+gupta2025endive)\\\\n# - background: 6 (+nakash2026divert)\\\\n# - high relevance: 15, medium: 13, low: 1\\\\n#\\\\n# Plan B scan notes (2026-05-13):\\\\n# - 4 new papers added specifically for Plan B empirical analysis framing\\\\n# - lee2024dpo_toxicity: mechanistic explanation for FM4 (DPO+BCP reversal)\\\\n# - gupta2026intra_fairness: external evidence for FM2 (cross-dimension interference) + FM4 (spillover)\\\\n# - basil2025playing_pretend + hu2026expert_persona: external evidence for FM1 (completion collapse via persona prompting)\\\\n# - No direct competitor found for BCP failure mode analysis — novelty gap confirmed\\\\n# - Also noted but not added:\\\\n#   - ϕ-DPO (2602.22601): Fairness DPO for multimodal continual learning, different domain\\\\n#   - Linguistic Stereotypes (2603.18729): SAE vs AAE bias in multi-agent architectures\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 27761, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EMzjXQYSrSW32TNMFn3MVt","session_id":"2e0fab31-3c97-44af-b8df-c308847e2709","uuid":"52a1791b-2183-42bc-99d7-18ee2e79e622","timestamp":"2026-05-13T16:22:19.478Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xcb7pHFgxp3GusvNurrhx9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# FM Replication Analysis: qwen14b-bcp-001\n2\t\n3\t**Model**: Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct FP16 (TP=2)  \n4\t**Experiment**: qwen14b-bcp-001, 4 runs × 6 variants × 20 scenarios  \n5\t**Date**: 2026-05-13\n6\t\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t## Per-Variant Data (All 4 Runs)\n10\t\n11\t### Task Completion Rate\n12\t\n13\t| Variant | Run 1 | Run 2 | Run 3 | Run 4 | Mean |\n14\t|---------|-------|-------|-------|-------|------|\n15\t| uncalibrated_baseline | 0.737 | 0.550 | 0.579 | 0.600 | **0.616** |\n16\t| aave_target | 0.700 | 0.474 | 0.632 | 0.600 | **0.601** |\n17\t| indian_english_target | 0.650 | 0.600 | 0.842 | 0.650 | **0.686** |\n18\t| overcorrected | 0.750 | 0.500 | 0.600 | 0.650 | **0.625** |\n19\t| single_dim_question | 0.500 | 0.700 | 0.600 | 0.600 | **0.600** |\n20\t| single_dim_politeness | 0.600 | 0.800 | 0.474 | 0.750 | **0.656** |\n21\t\n22\t### Mean Politeness Rate\n23\t\n24\t| Variant | Run 1 | Run 2 | Run 3 | Run 4 | Mean |\n25\t|---------|-------|-------|-------|-------|------|\n26\t| uncalibrated_baseline | 0.470 | 0.588 | 0.497 | 0.536 | **0.523** |\n27\t| aave_target | 0.393 | 0.454 | 0.493 | 0.455 | **0.449** |\n28\t| indian_english_target | 0.462 | 0.442 | 0.427 | 0.444 | **0.444** |\n29\t| overcorrected | 0.453 | 0.419 | 0.466 | 0.467 | **0.451** |\n30\t| single_dim_question | 0.449 | 0.499 | 0.463 | 0.512 | **0.481** |\n31\t| single_dim_politeness | 0.489 | 0.438 | 0.456 | 0.403 | **0.447** |\n32\t\n33\t### Mean Question Rate\n34\t\n35\t| Variant | Run 1 | Run 2 | Run 3 | Run 4 | Mean |\n36\t|---------|-------|-------|-------|-------|------|\n37\t| uncalibrated_baseline | 0.037 | 0.035 | 0.054 | 0.041 | **0.042** |\n38\t| aave_target | 0.044 | 0.015 | 0.044 | 0.008 | **0.028** |\n39\t| indian_english_target | 0.038 | 0.023 | 0.027 | 0.018 | **0.027** |\n40\t| overcorrected | 0.015 | 0.028 | 0.010 | 0.011 | **0.016** |\n41\t| single_dim_question | 0.048 | 0.017 | 0.034 | 0.005 | **0.026** |\n42\t| single_dim_politeness | 0.018 | 0.023 | 0.016 | 0.021 | **0.020** |\n43\t\n44\t### Gate Results\n45\t\n46\t| Run | H1 | H2 | H3 |\n47\t|-----|----|----|-----|\n48\t| run_1 | PASS | FAIL | PASS |\n49\t| run_2 | PASS | PASS | PASS |\n50\t| run_3 | PASS | FAIL | PASS |\n51\t| run_4 | PASS | FAIL | PASS |\n52\t| **Aggregate** | **4/4** | **1/4** | **4/4** |\n53\t\n54\t---\n55\t\n56\t## FM1: Global Behavior Suppression\n57\t\n58\t**Definition**: BCP variant completion significantly lower than baseline (retention < 80%).\n59\t\n60\t| Variant | Mean Completion | Baseline (0.616) | Retention | FM1? |\n61\t|---------|----------------|-------------------|-----------|------|\n62\t| aave_target | 0.601 | 0.616 | **97.6%** | NO |\n63\t| indian_english_target | 0.686 | 0.616 | **111.2%** | NO |\n64\t| overcorrected | 0.625 | 0.616 | **101.4%** | NO |\n65\t\n66\t**判断: FM1 未复现。** 所有 BCP variant 的 completion 保持率 > 97%，没有行为抑制。14B 模型在接到 BCP prompt 后仍然正常完成任务。\n67\t\n68\t---\n69\t\n70\t## FM2: Cross-Dimension Interference\n71\t\n72\t**Definition**: Calibrating one dimension causes unintended degradation of another dimension.\n73\t\n74\t### single_dim_question → politeness impact\n75\t\n76\t| Metric | Baseline | single_dim_question | Change |\n77\t|--------|----------|-------------------|--------|\n78\t| Politeness | 0.523 | 0.481 | **-8.0%** (-4.2 pp) |\n79\t\n80\t### single_dim_politeness → question_rate impact\n81\t\n82\t| Metric | Baseline | single_dim_politeness | Change |\n83\t|--------|----------|---------------------|--------|\n84\t| Question Rate | 0.042 | 0.020 | **-52.8%** (-2.2 pp) |\n85\t\n86\t**判断: FM2 部分复现（弱）。**\n87\t- question→politeness 方向：politeness 下降 8%（4.2 pp），效应较弱。\n88\t- politeness→question 方向：question_rate 相对下降 53%，但基线本身仅 4.2%，绝对下降仅 2.2 pp，可能在噪声范围内。\n89\t- 两个方向均存在跨维度干扰，但绝对幅度远小于 7B。\n90\t\n91\t---\n92\t\n93\t## FM3: Overcorrection Rebound\n94\t\n95\t**Definition**: overcorrected variant 的 politeness 高于 baseline（模型被 overcorrection prompt \"激发\"出更高的 politeness）。\n96\t\n97\t| Metric | Baseline | Overcorrected | Change |\n98\t|--------|----------|---------------|--------|\n99\t| Politeness | 0.523 | 0.451 | **-13.6%** (-7.1 pp) |\n100\t\n101\t**判断: FM3 未复现。** overcorrected variant 的 politeness 低于 baseline（-7.1 pp），方向与 FM3 预测相反。14B 模型不会因 overcorrection prompt 产生反弹效应，反而略微降低 politeness。\n102\t\n103\t---\n104\t\n105\t## 与 7B 对比\n106\t\n107\t| Failure Mode | 7B (mvp-004) | 14B (qwen14b-bcp-001) | 模式一致? |\n108\t|-------------|-------------|----------------------|----------|\n109\t| **FM1: Global Behavior Suppression** | 严重复现：AAVE 10%, Indian 15% vs baseline 36.8%（retention ~27-41%） | 未复现：所有 variant retention > 97% | **否** |\n110\t| **FM2: Cross-Dimension Interference** | 显著复现 | 弱复现：-8.0% politeness, -2.2pp question rate | **部分** |\n111\t| **FM3: Overcorrection Rebound** | 复现：overcorrected politeness > baseline | 未复现：overcorrected politeness < baseline (-7.1pp) | **否** |\n112\t| **H2 (Calibration Accuracy)** | FAIL | FAIL (3/4 runs) | **是** |\n113\t| **Mean Completion** | ~25% | ~61.6% | 14B >> 7B |\n114\t\n115\t---\n116\t\n117\t## 关键发现\n118\t\n119\t14B 模型的 BCP 校准失败机制与 7B **根本不同**：\n120\t\n121\t1. **7B 失败模式：灾难性崩溃。** BCP prompt 导致模型\"冻结\"——无法完成任务（completion 暴跌至 10-15%）、跨维度严重干扰、overcorrection 反弹。模型被 BCP prompt 严重干扰。\n122\t\n123\t2. **14B 失败模式：选择性忽略。** 模型正常完成任务（completion ~62%），但 BCP prompt 几乎没有改变行为指标。所有 variant 的 politeness 和 question_rate 都接近 baseline。模型不是被 BCP \"破坏\"了，而是**无视**了 BCP 校准指令。\n124\t\n125\t3. **共同点**：两种 scale 都 H2 FAIL——BCP 都未能成功校准目标行为维度。但失败原因不同：7B 是\"过度响应导致崩溃\"，14B 是\"不够响应导致无效\"。\n126\t\n127\t4. **论文启示**：这揭示了一个 scale-dependent failure mode transition。BCP 作为校准方法在两个 scale 上都失败，但机制相反——这是一个重要的 finding，说明 BCP 的问题不仅仅是\"prompt 设计不好\"，而是方法论层面的缺陷。\n128\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BtFwzKw5zaSQuQYxW6za5c","session_id":"2e0fab31-3c97-44af-b8df-c308847e2709","uuid":"99bbe24a-10af-4192-bb28-88c113cb8687","timestamp":"2026-05-13T16:22:19.286Z"}
