{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HZDsyiYRR5R3jxrHugJ9Ah","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260513T153032\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: borderline accept\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-13T16:19:58.774936+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report — Consolidated Roundtable\n9\t\n10\t**Paper**: \"Why Calibration Fails: A Taxonomy of Failure Modes in Dialect-Aware LLM User Simulation\"\n11\t**Venue**: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n12\t**Review Mode**: Full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)\n13\t**Date**: 2026-05-13\n14\t\n15\t---\n16\t\n17\t## Paper Summary\n18\t\n19\tThe paper investigates why calibration of LLM-based user simulators fails when attempting to correct dialect-dependent behavioral biases (AAVE, Indian English vs Standard American English). Two calibration strategies — Behavioral Calibration Prompting (BCP, inference-time) and Direct Preference Optimization (DPO, training-time) — are tested on Qwen2.5-7B/14B-Instruct in the τ-bench retail domain. Both fail. The authors propose a four-mechanism taxonomy of failure modes (FM1–FM4), demonstrate that all four are more severe at 7B than 14B via bootstrap CIs, and articulate a \"safety–efficacy gap\": scaling eliminates destructive side effects without improving calibration effectiveness.\n20\t\n21\t---\n22\t\n23\t## Reviewer Scores\n24\t\n25\t| Dimension | R1 (Methods+Exp) | R2 (Novelty+RW) | Consensus |\n26\t|-----------|:-:|:-:|:-:|\n27\t| Soundness | 3.0 | 3.0 | 3.0 |\n28\t| Excitement | 2.5 | 3.0 | 2.5 |\n29\t| Reproducibility | 3 | 3 | 3 |\n30\t| Overall | 3.0 | 3.0 | **3.0** |\n31\t| Confidence | 4 | 4 | 4 |\n32\t\n33\t**Consensus Overall: 3.0 — Borderline Findings Accept**\n34\t\n35\t---\n36\t\n37\t## Consolidated Strengths\n38\t\n39\t1. **FM4 (DPO–BCP interaction reversal) is genuinely novel.** The finding that DPO training reverses BCP calibration direction — with 10/12 directional consistency (p=0.006) at 7B and statistically significant attenuation at 14B (14.7pp, CI [8.1, 21.6]) — is the paper's most compelling contribution. No prior work has documented this interaction effect in calibration contexts.\n40\t\n41\t2. **Well-defined evaluation framework.** The H1/H2/H3 hypothesis structure with pre-specified thresholds (≥5% sensitivity, ≥30% gap reduction, ≥80% completion retention) provides a clear, falsifiable framework. This is a good methodological model for negative-results papers.\n42\t\n43\t3. **Appropriate and rigorous statistical methodology.** Non-parametric bootstrap with BCa correction (B=10,000) is well-suited for the small-n, non-normal distributions in this study. All core claims are backed by CIs that exclude zero. The sign test for FM4 directional consistency is correctly applied.\n44\t\n45\t4. **Honest reporting of limitations.** The question_rate arm is transparently reported as descriptive with absolute values due to low base rate (0.044). The paper acknowledges the Qwen-only limitation upfront and explains why Llama/Mistral controls failed (5%/0% completion).\n46\t\n47\t5. **Multi-run replication for key claims.** FM3 and FM4 are validated across 4 independent runs, and the 14B DPO experiment (plan_006) uses 4 runs × 6 variants × 20 tasks = 480 episodes. The cross-scale comparison has a reasonable statistical backbone.\n48\t\n49\t6. **Scale-dependent analysis adds value.** Showing that all four failure mechanisms have capacity-dependent severity, with different patterns (FM1/FM3 disappear; FM2/FM4 attenuate), is informative for calibration research even if the \"taxonomy\" framing is too strong.\n50\t\n51\t---\n52\t\n53\t## Consolidated Weaknesses\n54\t\n55\t### Major\n56\t\n57\t1. **FM1 and FM2 lack run-to-run replication (single-run, n=19–20).** Both FM1 (global suppression) and FM2 (cross-dimension interference) rest on single-run data with small samples. Bootstrap CIs quantify within-run uncertainty but cannot capture run-to-run variance. FM3 and FM4 benefit from 4-run replication; the same standard should apply to FM1 and FM2 before they are presented as robust \"mechanisms.\" *[R1]*\n58\t\n59\t2. **FM1 and FM2 are relabeled known phenomena.** FM1 is explicitly described as \"a task-oriented instantiation\" of the persona–accuracy trade-off (Basil et al. 2025, Hu et al. 2026). FM2 parallels bias spillover (Gupta et al. 2026). The \"first taxonomy\" claim oversells the contribution — it is more accurately an organizing framework that combines two known effects with two novel observations (FM3, FM4) in a new context. *[R2]*\n60\t\n61\t3. **Single model family (Qwen2.5) undermines taxonomy generality.** A \"taxonomy\" implicitly claims generality. With results from only Qwen2.5-7B and 14B, the four failure modes could be Qwen-specific artifacts. The paper acknowledges this but the framing doesn't match the evidence scope. The title should be hedged (e.g., \"Toward a Taxonomy\" or explicitly scoped to Qwen2.5). *[R1+R2]*\n62\t\n63\t4. **Safety–efficacy gap is overstated from two scale points.** Drawing a general principle (\"scaling changes how calibration fails, not whether it fails\") from {7B, 14B} in one model family is a stretch. The relationship could be non-monotonic, could reverse at larger scales, or could differ across families. This needs much stronger caveats or should be presented as a hypothesis rather than a finding. *[R1+R2]*\n64\t\n65\t5. **Significant missing related work weakens positioning.** *[R2]*\n66\t   - **Multi-objective alignment**: Rame et al. (2024, \"Rewarded soups\"), Yang et al. (2024, \"Rewards-in-context\") — FM2 is fundamentally a multi-objective problem with existing partial solutions.\n67\t   - **Controllable text generation**: FUDGE (Yang & Klein, 2021), PPLM (Dathathri et al., 2020), DExperts (Liu et al., 2021) — per-dimension control methods could address FM2.\n68\t   - **Scaling laws for alignment**: Bai et al. (2022, \"Training a helpful and harmless assistant\"), McKenzie et al. (2023, \"Inverse scaling\") — relevant to the scale-dependency claims.\n69\t   - The discussion mentions \"constrained decoding [Khan et al., 2026]\" but this is a 2026 preprint and the controllable generation literature has deeper prior work.\n70\t\n71\t6. **FM4 baseline confound.** The FM4 reversal compares base BCP (completion ~36.8%) vs DPO+BCP (completion ~60%). These conditions have very different underlying distributions, which means the politeness gap comparison is potentially confounded — models that complete more tasks may exhibit systematically different politeness patterns simply from seeing more diverse dialogue states. The paper does not control for this. *[R1]*\n72\t\n73\t### Minor\n74\t\n75\t7. **H2 threshold (≥30%) is unjustified.** The paper doesn't motivate why 30% is the right threshold. The best observed gap reduction is 27.6% (CI [18.3, 35.2]) — the CI actually includes 30%. The \"calibration fails\" narrative is sensitive to this threshold choice. A threshold sensitivity analysis would strengthen the claims. *[R1]*\n76\t\n77\t8. **DPO preference pair construction is underspecified.** The paper states 1,434 \"dialect-calibrated preference pairs\" but doesn't explain the construction process. Were preferred responses human-written? LLM-generated? How was \"appropriate dialect-matched behavioral rate\" operationalized? This limits reproducibility. *[R1]*\n78\t\n79\t9. **Possible author attribution error.** R2 flagged that \"Gupta et al. [2026]\" for arXiv:2602.16438 may have incorrect first-author attribution — the paper at that arXiv ID may have a different first author (Paraschou). This should be verified. *[R2]*\n80\t\n81\t10. **No figures.** The paper is entirely tables and text. Visualization of the scale-dependent failure patterns (e.g., a heatmap or forest plot) would substantially improve readability and impact. *[R1+R2]*\n82\t\n83\t---\n84\t\n85\t## Questions for Authors\n86\t\n87\t1. **Can you provide multi-run replication for FM1 and FM2?** What is the run-to-run variance for the single-dimension conditions? (If available from additional runs not reported.)\n88\t\n89\t2. **How were the 1,434 DPO preference pairs constructed?** Were preferred responses generated by prompting the base model with dialect-matched behavioral targets? Human-curated? What quality control was applied?\n90\t\n91\t3. **Have you tested the H2 threshold sensitivity?** At what threshold does H2 start passing? The CI for best gap reduction [18.3, 35.2] suggests that at a 20% threshold, H2 might pass in some conditions.\n92\t\n93\t4. **Can you control for the completion-rate confound in FM4?** For instance, by comparing politeness gaps only among completed episodes, or by matching on completion status.\n94\t\n95\t5. **Why no controllable generation baselines?** FUDGE or DExperts-style approaches could provide per-dimension behavioral control that avoids FM2 by design. Even if infeasible to run, discussing why these are excluded would strengthen the paper.\n96\t\n97\t---\n98\t\n99\t## Missing Experiments or Ablations\n100\t\n101\t- Multi-run replication for FM1 and FM2 (4 runs each, matching FM3/FM4 rigor)\n102\t- DPO-only evaluation *without* BCP (isolate DPO's standalone calibration effect)\n103\t- BCP prompt intensity ablation (does graduated vs binary targeting affect FM3?)\n104\t- H2 threshold sensitivity analysis\n105\t- Completion-matched FM4 analysis (compare gaps only among completed episodes)\n106\t- At least one non-Qwen model with sufficient tool-calling ability (even if limited to BCP-only)\n107\t\n108\t---\n109\t\n110\t## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n111\t\n112\tComparing against the full experiment design (review_context.yaml):\n113\t\n114\t| Experiment | Status | Notes |\n115\t|-----------|--------|-------|\n116\t| Bootstrap CI (plan_005) | ✅ Fully covered | Table 2, all 5 comparisons |\n117\t| 14B DPO+BCP (plan_006) | ✅ Fully covered | Tables 3–5, 480 episodes |\n118\t\n119\tBoth planned experiments are well-reflected in the paper. The paper covers what was planned; the gaps identified above are beyond the original experiment design.\n120\t\n121\t---\n122\t\n123\t## Roundtable Discussion Summary\n124\t\n125\t### Points of Agreement\n126\t- FM4 is the strongest and most novel contribution; its scale-dependency analysis is the paper's highlight\n127\t- Bootstrap methodology is appropriate and well-executed\n128\t- Single model family is the biggest external validity concern\n129\t- Safety–efficacy gap needs stronger caveats or reframing as hypothesis\n130\t- The paper reads as slightly overselling known effects (FM1, FM2) as novel taxonomy entries\n131\t\n132\t### Points of Productive Disagreement\n133\t- R1 weighted the FM4 baseline confound more heavily (potentially undermining the strongest claim); R2 considered this less fatal given the sign test's robustness to baseline shifts. Consensus: it warrants acknowledgment and ideally a completion-matched analysis, but likely doesn't overturn FM4.\n134\t- R2 was more concerned about missing related work (multi-objective alignment, controllable generation) implying that FM2 has known solutions; R1 noted the τ-bench tool-calling setting is sufficiently distinct that those methods may not directly apply. Consensus: the paper should discuss these methods and explain why they don't straightforwardly transfer.\n135\t\n136\t### Accept/Reject Reasoning\n137\t**For acceptance (Findings):** FM4 is genuinely novel with strong statistical support. The scale-dependent failure analysis is informative. The H1/H2/H3 framework is well-constructed. Negative results with clear characterization have value for the community. The experimental execution (especially plan_005 and plan_006) is solid for the claims actually supported.\n138\t\n139\t**Against acceptance:** The \"taxonomy\" framing is stronger than the evidence warrants — two of four mechanisms are known phenomena, and the single-family evidence doesn't support the generality that \"taxonomy\" implies. Key experiments (FM1/FM2 multi-run) are missing. The safety–efficacy gap, while intriguing, is underpowered.\n140\t\n141\t**Consensus recommendation:** Borderline Findings accept (3.0). The paper would benefit from (1) reframing from \"taxonomy\" to \"empirical characterization,\" (2) multi-run FM1/FM2 replication, (3) expanded related work, and (4) explicit caveats on the safety–efficacy gap. If the authors can address items 1, 3, and 4 in a revision (items requiring no new experiments), the paper clears the Findings bar comfortably.\n142\t\n143\t---\n144\t\n145\t## Final Assessment\n146\t\n147\t**Overall Score: 3.0 (Borderline Findings Accept)**\n148\t\n149\tThe paper presents a well-executed empirical investigation of calibration failures in dialect-aware LLM user simulation. FM4 (DPO–BCP interaction reversal) is a genuinely novel finding with rigorous statistical validation. The scale-dependent analysis across 7B and 14B is informative. However, the \"taxonomy\" framing oversells the novelty (FM1/FM2 are known phenomena in new context), the single model family limits generalizability, and several experimental gaps (FM1/FM2 multi-run replication, FM4 baseline confound control) prevent a confident accept. With moderate revisions — primarily reframing and expanded related work — this would be a solid Findings paper.\n150\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BtFwzKw5zaSQuQYxW6za5c","session_id":"2e0fab31-3c97-44af-b8df-c308847e2709","uuid":"0735949c-7be2-493c-9fec-6781bb42495c","timestamp":"2026-05-13T16:22:29.902Z"}
