{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EMzjXQYSrSW32TNMFn3MVt","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThe paper proposes a \"taxonomy\" of four failure modes (FM1--FM4) when calibrating LLM user simulators for dialect-aware behavioral rates, using BCP (prompt-based) and DPO (training-based) strategies on Qwen2.5-7B/14B in tau-bench retail. FM3 (overcorrection rebound) and FM4 (DPO-BCP interaction reversal) are genuinely novel observations. FM1 and FM2 are acknowledged restatements of known phenomena (persona-accuracy tradeoff, bias spillover) in a new setting. The related work section is thin (9 references in the submitted draft) and misses several important lines of work that would contextualize the contributions more accurately.\n\n### Strengths\n1. **FM4 (DPO-BCP interaction reversal) is a genuinely novel finding.** No prior work documents that DPO training can reverse the direction of prompt-based calibration. The 10/12 directional consistency with p=0.006 at 7B, and the statistically significant attenuation at 14B (14.7pp, CI [8.1, 21.6]), constitute a real empirical discovery. This is the paper's strongest novelty claim and it holds up.\n\n2. **FM3 (overcorrection rebound) is novel and non-obvious.** The finding that extreme calibration targets cause opposite-direction shifts (politeness *increases* when targeted for extreme reduction) is not directly predicted by any prior work cited. The 4/4 run consistency at 7B and clean disappearance at 14B adds a scale-dependent dimension that is informative.\n\n3. **The paper honestly acknowledges where its observations parallel prior work.** FM1 is explicitly linked to Basil et al. 2025 and Hu et al. 2026; FM2 to Gupta et al. 2026. This intellectual honesty is commendable and relatively rare. The contribution is clearly framed as documenting these phenomena in a new context (multi-turn agent dialogue with tool-calling), not as discovering them from scratch.\n\n4. **The scale-dependent failure severity analysis is a meaningful organizational contribution.** Showing that FM1/FM3 are capacity-gated (disappear at 14B) while FM2/FM4 are merely attenuated provides a useful structuring of the failure landscape, even if the two-point scale comparison is limited.\n\n5. **The paper situates itself well against the closest concurrent work** (Sim et al. 2026 on sim2real gap, Seshadri et al. 2026 on dialect-dependent calibration errors). The differentiation is clear: those works diagnose the problem, this paper attempts and systematically documents the failure of two interventions.\n\n### Weaknesses\n1. **The \"taxonomy\" framing significantly overclaims novelty.** A taxonomy implies a comprehensive, generalizable classification system. Two of four entries (FM1, FM2) are self-acknowledged instances of known phenomena. The evidence comes from one model family on one benchmark. This is more accurately an \"empirical characterization\" or \"case study\" of calibration failures. The word \"taxonomy\" appears in the title, abstract, contributions, and conclusion -- it should be downgraded throughout or explicitly qualified (e.g., \"toward a taxonomy\" or \"an empirical taxonomy within Qwen2.5\").\n\n2. **The related work section is seriously underweight for EMNLP.** The submitted draft has only 9 references. The literature map (29 papers) shows the authors are aware of much more relevant work but chose not to cite it. Major gaps:\n\n   - **Controllable text generation literature**: FUDGE (Yang & Klein, 2021), PPLM (Dathathri et al., 2020), DExperts (Liu et al., 2021), GeDi (Krause et al., 2021). FM2 (cross-dimension interference) is fundamentally a multi-attribute controllable generation problem. These methods offer per-dimension control that could avoid FM2 by design. Not discussing them makes the paper appear unaware of an entire subfield that directly addresses one of its core failure modes.\n\n   - **Multi-objective alignment**: Rame et al. (2024, \"Rewarded soups\"), Yang et al. (2024, \"Rewards-in-context\"), Mudgal et al. (2024, \"Controlled decoding from LMs\"). FM2's cross-dimension interference is a known challenge in multi-objective optimization. The alignment community has proposed solutions the paper does not engage with.\n\n   - **Scaling laws for alignment behavior**: Bai et al. (2022, \"Training a helpful and harmless assistant from human feedback\"), McKenzie et al. (2023, \"Inverse scaling\"), Wei et al. (2022, \"Emergent abilities of large language models\"). The safety-efficacy gap claim -- that scaling resolves harms but not effectiveness -- is a scaling behavior claim that should be situated against existing scaling literature. The observation that larger models \"selectively ignore\" prompts while smaller models are overwhelmed may relate to known phenomena around instruction-following capacity and scale.\n\n   - **Broader dialect/sociolinguistic NLP**: The literature map includes Faisal et al. (2024, DialectBench), Lin et al. (2025, ReDial), Joshi et al. (2025, dialect NLP survey), Haq & Saldias (2026), Gupta et al. (2025, EnDive) -- none of which appear in the submitted draft. For a paper about \"dialect-aware\" simulation, citing zero dialect NLP benchmarks or surveys is a notable gap.\n\n   - **User simulation beyond tau-bench**: Abdulhai et al. (2025, multi-turn RL for persona consistency), Naous et al. (2025, User LMs), Sekulic et al. (2024, DAUS), Ahmad et al. (2025, user diversity simulation) -- all in the literature map but not cited. These provide context for the difficulty of behavioral control in user simulation.\n\n   - **DPO failure modes and alternatives**: Beyond Lee et al. (2024), there is substantial work on DPO limitations: Azar et al. (2024, \"A general theoretical paradigm to understand learning from human feedback\"), Xu et al. (2024, \"Some things are more CRINGE than others\"), Ethayarajh et al. (2024, \"KTO\"). These would strengthen the FM4 discussion.\n\n3. **The \"safety-efficacy gap\" framing is presented as a novel conceptual contribution but is arguably a restatement of known scaling behavior.** The observation that larger models resist prompt perturbation (both harmful and beneficial) is implicit in the instruction-following and prompt sensitivity literature. Larger models are known to be more robust to prompt variations (Lu et al., 2022, \"Fantastically ordered prompts\"), which means they are harder to perturb in *any* direction. Framing this as a \"gap\" implies the two properties (safety and efficacy) are separable in a way the evidence does not clearly support -- they may both stem from the same underlying prompt robustness.\n\n4. **FM4's novelty is partially predictable from Lee et al. (2024).** Lee et al. show DPO alignment is \"fragile\" and can be reversed. It is a small step to predict that combining a fragile DPO alignment with a competing prompt signal could produce interference or reversal. The paper acknowledges this connection but still claims FM4 as a novel \"discovery.\" The genuine novelty is the *specific empirical demonstration* in a calibration context and the quantified scale-dependency, not the conceptual mechanism.\n\n5. **No engagement with why constrained decoding / per-dimension reward approaches were not tried.** The Discussion mentions \"constrained decoding (Khan et al., 2026)\" as future work, but the controllable generation literature has 5+ years of relevant methods. The paper would be much stronger if it explained why FUDGE-style or reward-model-guided decoding was excluded -- is it a computational constraint? Incompatibility with tool-calling? This would also sharpen the contribution: if existing controllable generation methods cannot handle multi-turn tool-calling dialogue, that itself is worth stating explicitly.\n\n6. **The Overcorrected variant is underspecified as a novelty claim.** FM3 relies on the \"Overcorrected\" variant -- a condition where extreme behavioral targets are set. But the paper does not clearly define what \"overcorrected\" means quantitatively (what target rates?), making it hard to assess whether the rebound is surprising or an expected artifact of an unreasonable target. If the target politeness rate was set to 0% or 5%, a model rebounding to 57% might simply reflect the model's inability to produce coherent customer service dialogue with near-zero politeness.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n1. Why does the submitted draft cite only 9 of the 29 papers in your literature map? Several omitted papers (DialectBench, ReDial, EnDive, Abdulhai et al., Naous et al.) are marked \"high relevance\" in your own literature map and directly strengthen the paper's positioning.\n\n2. Have you considered whether FM2 (cross-dimension interference) has known solutions in the controllable generation literature (FUDGE, DExperts, PPLM)? If so, why were these not discussed as alternative approaches? If they are infeasible in the tau-bench tool-calling setting, stating this explicitly would strengthen the contribution.\n\n3. What are the exact target behavioral rates for the \"Overcorrected\" variant? Is the FM3 rebound an artifact of setting an unrealistically extreme target, or does it occur even with moderate-but-directionally-strong targets?\n\n4. The safety-efficacy gap is framed as a finding, but could it be an artifact of the specific model family? Qwen2.5-14B may simply have stronger instruction-following alignment that resists *all* system prompt modifications. Have you tested whether the 14B model responds to *any* behavioral prompt modifications (not just BCP calibration)?\n\n5. Can you clarify the relationship between your FM4 and the general prediction from Lee et al. (2024) that DPO alignment is fragile and reversible? What is the incremental novelty beyond confirming their mechanism in a new setting?\n\n6. The paper claims \"first taxonomy of calibration failure modes for LLM user simulators\" -- have you verified there is no prior work on calibration failures in dialogue systems more broadly (not just LLM-based)? The dialogue systems community has decades of work on user simulation calibration.\n\n### Minor Issues\n1. Reference formatting: \"Sim2Real Benchmark Team (2026)\" for arXiv 2603.11245 appears to be a placeholder -- the actual authors are Zhou, Sun, Xu, Chen, Radev. Similarly, \"Gupta et al. (2026)\" for arXiv 2602.16438 may have incorrect first-author attribution (the paper's first author appears to be Paraschou).\n\n2. The bibliography uses inconsistent formatting -- some entries have full author lists, others use \"et al.\" in the bib entry itself rather than letting natbib handle abbreviation.\n\n3. The claim \"All four mechanisms are more severe at 7B than 14B\" in the abstract should be qualified -- FM2 is \"attenuated\" at 14B, not \"absent,\" and the 14B FM2 data with DPO shows it persists. The abstract's phrasing is technically correct but could mislead readers into thinking all mechanisms disappear at 14B.\n\n4. The paper would benefit significantly from at least one figure -- a forest plot of the bootstrap CIs or a heatmap of the severity matrix would improve accessibility.\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: 3/5\n- Excitement: 2.5/5\n- Overall: 2.5/5\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\n### Detailed Assessment\n\n**On novelty.** The paper's novelty is unevenly distributed across its four claimed contributions. FM4 (DPO-BCP interaction reversal) is genuinely novel and well-supported -- no prior work demonstrates this specific interaction effect, and the scale-dependent attenuation adds a dimension that goes beyond simply confirming Lee et al. (2024). FM3 (overcorrection rebound) is also novel, though its practical significance depends on how the Overcorrected variant's target rates were chosen. FM1 and FM2, however, are self-acknowledged instances of known phenomena (persona-accuracy tradeoff, bias spillover) applied in a new context. The \"taxonomy\" framing elevates these four observations into a classification system that implies generality the evidence does not support. A more accurate framing would be \"an empirical characterization of calibration failures in Qwen2.5, revealing two novel mechanisms (FM3, FM4) alongside two known ones (FM1, FM2) in a multi-turn tool-calling setting.\" This is still a valid contribution for Findings, but the current framing sets expectations the paper cannot meet.\n\n**On related work.** The related work section is the paper's most significant weakness from my review perspective. Nine references for an EMNLP submission is far below the expected standard -- comparable empirical analysis papers at ACL/EMNLP typically cite 30-50 works. The authors' own literature map contains 29 papers, many marked high-relevance, suggesting this is a drafting gap rather than a literature awareness gap. The most damaging omissions are in controllable text generation (FUDGE, PPLM, DExperts) and multi-objective alignment (Rewarded Soups, Controlled Decoding), because these literatures contain *existing partial solutions* to FM2. Without engaging with these methods, the paper risks appearing to present cross-dimension interference as an unsolved problem when the field has proposed (if not fully validated) approaches. The dialect NLP omissions (DialectBench, ReDial, EnDive) are also notable for a paper with \"Dialect-Aware\" in the title. Expanding the related work to 25-30 references and engaging substantively with controllable generation and multi-objective alignment would meaningfully improve the paper's positioning and credibility.\n\n**On contribution framing.** The gap between claimed and demonstrated generality is the paper's central tension. The title says \"taxonomy,\" the contributions say \"first,\" and the abstract says \"actionable constraints for future calibration research\" -- but all evidence comes from one model family on one benchmark with modest sample sizes. The internal decision log (D015) already acknowledges this tension. I recommend the authors reframe from \"taxonomy\" to \"empirical characterization,\" scope the title to Qwen2.5 or add \"toward,\" and present the safety-efficacy gap as a hypothesis warranting further investigation rather than an established finding. With these adjustments and an expanded related work section, the paper's genuine contributions (FM3, FM4, scale-dependent analysis) would be appropriately highlighted without overclaiming."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: acda71765a4f0fbe1 (use SendMessage with to: 'acda71765a4f0fbe1' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 62140\ntool_uses: 16\nduration_ms: 129846</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2e0fab31-3c97-44af-b8df-c308847e2709","uuid":"eb3cbf34-42a4-4c7e-9913-a02167f1bb54","timestamp":"2026-05-13T16:24:19.261Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 Findings submission. Your focus areas are **novelty and related work**.\n\n## Venue Scoring (ACL Rolling Review)\n- Soundness (1–5): 5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Poor, 1=Major Issues\n- Excitement (1–5): 5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Moderate, 2=Low, 1=None\n- Overall Assessment (1–5 with half-points): 5=Award (top 2.5%), 4=Strong Accept, 3.5=Lean Accept, 3=Borderline, 2.5=Lean Reject, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1.5=Resubmit later, 1=Do not resubmit\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Why Calibration Fails: A Taxonomy of Failure Modes in Dialect-Aware LLM User Simulation\"\n\nThe paper investigates why behavioral calibration of LLM user simulators fails. Two strategies—BCP (prompt-based calibration) and DPO (training-time)—are evaluated on Qwen2.5-7B/14B in τ-bench retail domain. The paper identifies:\n- FM1: Global behavior suppression (BCP collapses completion to 52.7% of baseline)\n- FM2: Cross-dimension interference (targeting one dimension shifts others by -23.7%)\n- FM3: Overcorrection rebound (extreme targets cause opposite-direction drift, 4/4 runs)\n- FM4/Interaction: DPO reverses BCP calibration direction (10/12 variant×run)\n- Central finding: safety-efficacy gap—7B→14B scaling removes destructive side effects but doesn't improve calibration\n\n## Related Work Section (from paper)\nThe paper covers three areas:\n1. **LLM User Simulation**: Cites Yao et al. 2024 (τ-bench), Sim et al. 2026 (simulator-human gap), Seshadri et al. 2026 (AAVE/Indian English worst calibration)\n2. **Persona Prompting and Accuracy**: Basil et al. 2025 (MMLU drops 71.6%→66.3%), Hu et al. 2026 (up to 30pp degradation)\n3. **Alignment Side Effects**: Gupta et al. 2026 (bias spillover), Lee et al. 2024 (DPO bypasses capabilities)\n\nTotal references: 9 (very sparse for a venue like EMNLP)\n\n## Claimed Contributions\n1. Taxonomy of 3 failure mechanisms (FM1-FM3) validated across 4 runs\n2. DPO-BCP interaction effect at 7B (10/12 variant×run gap expansions)\n3. Scale-dependent failure severity revealing safety-efficacy gap\n4. Actionable constraints for future calibration research\n\n## Key Limitations Acknowledged\n- Single model family (Qwen2.5 only; Llama/Mistral floor-level completion)\n- DPO-BCP interaction only at 7B (14B DPO not feasible)\n- Single task domain (τ-bench retail)\n- Small sample size (n=20 tasks per variant per run)\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a thorough review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**: Is the \"taxonomy of calibration failure modes\" genuinely novel? FM1 is acknowledged as paralleling persona-accuracy tradeoff (Basil et al., Hu et al.). FM2 parallels bias spillover (Gupta et al.). FM3 (overcorrection rebound) is claimed as novel. Is the DPO-BCP interaction genuinely new or predictable from Lee et al. 2024?\n\n2. **Related work completeness**: Only 9 references is very thin. Are there missing lines of work? Consider:\n   - Broader calibration/alignment literature\n   - Dialect/sociolinguistic NLP work\n   - Multi-turn agent evaluation beyond τ-bench\n   - Instruction-following capacity literature\n   - Other DPO/RLHF failure mode work\n   - Constrained generation/decoding literature (only Khan et al. 2026 mentioned in passing)\n\n3. **Contribution framing**: Is \"taxonomy\" an appropriate framing? The paper has 3 failure modes + 1 interaction effect on a single benchmark with a single model family. Is this a taxonomy or a case study?\n\n4. **Safety-efficacy gap**: Is this framing novel and well-supported? Or is it a restatement of known scaling behavior (larger models are harder to steer with prompts)?\n\n5. **Positioning relative to concurrent work**: How well does the paper situate itself vs. Sim et al. 2026, Seshadri et al. 2026, and the persona prompting literature?\n\n6. **Generalizability of claims**: The abstract says \"our taxonomy provides actionable constraints for future calibration research\" but all evidence is from one model family on one benchmark. How generalizable are the findings?\n\nOutput your review in this format:\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n\n### Detailed Assessment\n[2-3 paragraphs on novelty/related work quality]\n```\n","agentId":"acda71765a4f0fbe1","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThe paper proposes a \"taxonomy\" of four failure modes (FM1--FM4) when calibrating LLM user simulators for dialect-aware behavioral rates, using BCP (prompt-based) and DPO (training-based) strategies on Qwen2.5-7B/14B in tau-bench retail. FM3 (overcorrection rebound) and FM4 (DPO-BCP interaction reversal) are genuinely novel observations. FM1 and FM2 are acknowledged restatements of known phenomena (persona-accuracy tradeoff, bias spillover) in a new setting. The related work section is thin (9 references in the submitted draft) and misses several important lines of work that would contextualize the contributions more accurately.\n\n### Strengths\n1. **FM4 (DPO-BCP interaction reversal) is a genuinely novel finding.** No prior work documents that DPO training can reverse the direction of prompt-based calibration. The 10/12 directional consistency with p=0.006 at 7B, and the statistically significant attenuation at 14B (14.7pp, CI [8.1, 21.6]), constitute a real empirical discovery. This is the paper's strongest novelty claim and it holds up.\n\n2. **FM3 (overcorrection rebound) is novel and non-obvious.** The finding that extreme calibration targets cause opposite-direction shifts (politeness *increases* when targeted for extreme reduction) is not directly predicted by any prior work cited. The 4/4 run consistency at 7B and clean disappearance at 14B adds a scale-dependent dimension that is informative.\n\n3. **The paper honestly acknowledges where its observations parallel prior work.** FM1 is explicitly linked to Basil et al. 2025 and Hu et al. 2026; FM2 to Gupta et al. 2026. This intellectual honesty is commendable and relatively rare. The contribution is clearly framed as documenting these phenomena in a new context (multi-turn agent dialogue with tool-calling), not as discovering them from scratch.\n\n4. **The scale-dependent failure severity analysis is a meaningful organizational contribution.** Showing that FM1/FM3 are capacity-gated (disappear at 14B) while FM2/FM4 are merely attenuated provides a useful structuring of the failure landscape, even if the two-point scale comparison is limited.\n\n5. **The paper situates itself well against the closest concurrent work** (Sim et al. 2026 on sim2real gap, Seshadri et al. 2026 on dialect-dependent calibration errors). The differentiation is clear: those works diagnose the problem, this paper attempts and systematically documents the failure of two interventions.\n\n### Weaknesses\n1. **The \"taxonomy\" framing significantly overclaims novelty.** A taxonomy implies a comprehensive, generalizable classification system. Two of four entries (FM1, FM2) are self-acknowledged instances of known phenomena. The evidence comes from one model family on one benchmark. This is more accurately an \"empirical characterization\" or \"case study\" of calibration failures. The word \"taxonomy\" appears in the title, abstract, contributions, and conclusion -- it should be downgraded throughout or explicitly qualified (e.g., \"toward a taxonomy\" or \"an empirical taxonomy within Qwen2.5\").\n\n2. **The related work section is seriously underweight for EMNLP.** The submitted draft has only 9 references. The literature map (29 papers) shows the authors are aware of much more relevant work but chose not to cite it. Major gaps:\n\n   - **Controllable text generation literature**: FUDGE (Yang & Klein, 2021), PPLM (Dathathri et al., 2020), DExperts (Liu et al., 2021), GeDi (Krause et al., 2021). FM2 (cross-dimension interference) is fundamentally a multi-attribute controllable generation problem. These methods offer per-dimension control that could avoid FM2 by design. Not discussing them makes the paper appear unaware of an entire subfield that directly addresses one of its core failure modes.\n\n   - **Multi-objective alignment**: Rame et al. (2024, \"Rewarded soups\"), Yang et al. (2024, \"Rewards-in-context\"), Mudgal et al. (2024, \"Controlled decoding from LMs\"). FM2's cross-dimension interference is a known challenge in multi-objective optimization. The alignment community has proposed solutions the paper does not engage with.\n\n   - **Scaling laws for alignment behavior**: Bai et al. (2022, \"Training a helpful and harmless assistant from human feedback\"), McKenzie et al. (2023, \"Inverse scaling\"), Wei et al. (2022, \"Emergent abilities of large language models\"). The safety-efficacy gap claim -- that scaling resolves harms but not effectiveness -- is a scaling behavior claim that should be situated against existing scaling literature. The observation that larger models \"selectively ignore\" prompts while smaller models are overwhelmed may relate to known phenomena around instruction-following capacity and scale.\n\n   - **Broader dialect/sociolinguistic NLP**: The literature map includes Faisal et al. (2024, DialectBench), Lin et al. (2025, ReDial), Joshi et al. (2025, dialect NLP survey), Haq & Saldias (2026), Gupta et al. (2025, EnDive) -- none of which appear in the submitted draft. For a paper about \"dialect-aware\" simulation, citing zero dialect NLP benchmarks or surveys is a notable gap.\n\n   - **User simulation beyond tau-bench**: Abdulhai et al. (2025, multi-turn RL for persona consistency), Naous et al. (2025, User LMs), Sekulic et al. (2024, DAUS), Ahmad et al. (2025, user diversity simulation) -- all in the literature map but not cited. These provide context for the difficulty of behavioral control in user simulation.\n\n   - **DPO failure modes and alternatives**: Beyond Lee et al. (2024), there is substantial work on DPO limitations: Azar et al. (2024, \"A general theoretical paradigm to understand learning from human feedback\"), Xu et al. (2024, \"Some things are more CRINGE than others\"), Ethayarajh et al. (2024, \"KTO\"). These would strengthen the FM4 discussion.\n\n3. **The \"safety-efficacy gap\" framing is presented as a novel conceptual contribution but is arguably a restatement of known scaling behavior.** The observation that larger models resist prompt perturbation (both harmful and beneficial) is implicit in the instruction-following and prompt sensitivity literature. Larger models are known to be more robust to prompt variations (Lu et al., 2022, \"Fantastically ordered prompts\"), which means they are harder to perturb in *any* direction. Framing this as a \"gap\" implies the two properties (safety and efficacy) are separable in a way the evidence does not clearly support -- they may both stem from the same underlying prompt robustness.\n\n4. **FM4's novelty is partially predictable from Lee et al. (2024).** Lee et al. show DPO alignment is \"fragile\" and can be reversed. It is a small step to predict that combining a fragile DPO alignment with a competing prompt signal could produce interference or reversal. The paper acknowledges this connection but still claims FM4 as a novel \"discovery.\" The genuine novelty is the *specific empirical demonstration* in a calibration context and the quantified scale-dependency, not the conceptual mechanism.\n\n5. **No engagement with why constrained decoding / per-dimension reward approaches were not tried.** The Discussion mentions \"constrained decoding (Khan et al., 2026)\" as future work, but the controllable generation literature has 5+ years of relevant methods. The paper would be much stronger if it explained why FUDGE-style or reward-model-guided decoding was excluded -- is it a computational constraint? Incompatibility with tool-calling? This would also sharpen the contribution: if existing controllable generation methods cannot handle multi-turn tool-calling dialogue, that itself is worth stating explicitly.\n\n6. **The Overcorrected variant is underspecified as a novelty claim.** FM3 relies on the \"Overcorrected\" variant -- a condition where extreme behavioral targets are set. But the paper does not clearly define what \"overcorrected\" means quantitatively (what target rates?), making it hard to assess whether the rebound is surprising or an expected artifact of an unreasonable target. If the target politeness rate was set to 0% or 5%, a model rebounding to 57% might simply reflect the model's inability to produce coherent customer service dialogue with near-zero politeness.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n1. Why does the submitted draft cite only 9 of the 29 papers in your literature map? Several omitted papers (DialectBench, ReDial, EnDive, Abdulhai et al., Naous et al.) are marked \"high relevance\" in your own literature map and directly strengthen the paper's positioning.\n\n2. Have you considered whether FM2 (cross-dimension interference) has known solutions in the controllable generation literature (FUDGE, DExperts, PPLM)? If so, why were these not discussed as alternative approaches? If they are infeasible in the tau-bench tool-calling setting, stating this explicitly would strengthen the contribution.\n\n3. What are the exact target behavioral rates for the \"Overcorrected\" variant? Is the FM3 rebound an artifact of setting an unrealistically extreme target, or does it occur even with moderate-but-directionally-strong targets?\n\n4. The safety-efficacy gap is framed as a finding, but could it be an artifact of the specific model family? Qwen2.5-14B may simply have stronger instruction-following alignment that resists *all* system prompt modifications. Have you tested whether the 14B model responds to *any* behavioral prompt modifications (not just BCP calibration)?\n\n5. Can you clarify the relationship between your FM4 and the general prediction from Lee et al. (2024) that DPO alignment is fragile and reversible? What is the incremental novelty beyond confirming their mechanism in a new setting?\n\n6. The paper claims \"first taxonomy of calibration failure modes for LLM user simulators\" -- have you verified there is no prior work on calibration failures in dialogue systems more broadly (not just LLM-based)? The dialogue systems community has decades of work on user simulation calibration.\n\n### Minor Issues\n1. Reference formatting: \"Sim2Real Benchmark Team (2026)\" for arXiv 2603.11245 appears to be a placeholder -- the actual authors are Zhou, Sun, Xu, Chen, Radev. Similarly, \"Gupta et al. (2026)\" for arXiv 2602.16438 may have incorrect first-author attribution (the paper's first author appears to be Paraschou).\n\n2. The bibliography uses inconsistent formatting -- some entries have full author lists, others use \"et al.\" in the bib entry itself rather than letting natbib handle abbreviation.\n\n3. The claim \"All four mechanisms are more severe at 7B than 14B\" in the abstract should be qualified -- FM2 is \"attenuated\" at 14B, not \"absent,\" and the 14B FM2 data with DPO shows it persists. The abstract's phrasing is technically correct but could mislead readers into thinking all mechanisms disappear at 14B.\n\n4. The paper would benefit significantly from at least one figure -- a forest plot of the bootstrap CIs or a heatmap of the severity matrix would improve accessibility.\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: 3/5\n- Excitement: 2.5/5\n- Overall: 2.5/5\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\n### Detailed Assessment\n\n**On novelty.** The paper's novelty is unevenly distributed across its four claimed contributions. FM4 (DPO-BCP interaction reversal) is genuinely novel and well-supported -- no prior work demonstrates this specific interaction effect, and the scale-dependent attenuation adds a dimension that goes beyond simply confirming Lee et al. (2024). FM3 (overcorrection rebound) is also novel, though its practical significance depends on how the Overcorrected variant's target rates were chosen. FM1 and FM2, however, are self-acknowledged instances of known phenomena (persona-accuracy tradeoff, bias spillover) applied in a new context. The \"taxonomy\" framing elevates these four observations into a classification system that implies generality the evidence does not support. A more accurate framing would be \"an empirical characterization of calibration failures in Qwen2.5, revealing two novel mechanisms (FM3, FM4) alongside two known ones (FM1, FM2) in a multi-turn tool-calling setting.\" This is still a valid contribution for Findings, but the current framing sets expectations the paper cannot meet.\n\n**On related work.** The related work section is the paper's most significant weakness from my review perspective. Nine references for an EMNLP submission is far below the expected standard -- comparable empirical analysis papers at ACL/EMNLP typically cite 30-50 works. The authors' own literature map contains 29 papers, many marked high-relevance, suggesting this is a drafting gap rather than a literature awareness gap. The most damaging omissions are in controllable text generation (FUDGE, PPLM, DExperts) and multi-objective alignment (Rewarded Soups, Controlled Decoding), because these literatures contain *existing partial solutions* to FM2. Without engaging with these methods, the paper risks appearing to present cross-dimension interference as an unsolved problem when the field has proposed (if not fully validated) approaches. The dialect NLP omissions (DialectBench, ReDial, EnDive) are also notable for a paper with \"Dialect-Aware\" in the title. Expanding the related work to 25-30 references and engaging substantively with controllable generation and multi-objective alignment would meaningfully improve the paper's positioning and credibility.\n\n**On contribution framing.** The gap between claimed and demonstrated generality is the paper's central tension. The title says \"taxonomy,\" the contributions say \"first,\" and the abstract says \"actionable constraints for future calibration research\" -- but all evidence comes from one model family on one benchmark with modest sample sizes. The internal decision log (D015) already acknowledges this tension. I recommend the authors reframe from \"taxonomy\" to \"empirical characterization,\" scope the title to Qwen2.5 or add \"toward,\" and present the safety-efficacy gap as a hypothesis warranting further investigation rather than an established finding. With these adjustments and an expanded related work section, the paper's genuine contributions (FM3, FM4, scale-dependent analysis) would be appropriately highlighted without overclaiming."}],"totalDurationMs":129846,"totalTokens":62140,"totalToolUseCount":16,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":715,"cache_read_input_tokens":57955,"output_tokens":3467,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":715},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3467,"cache_read_input_tokens":57955,"cache_creation_input_tokens":715,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":715,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":3,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":10}}}
